1
00:03:19,090 --> 00:03:25,090
A Glowing Undersea Adventure!

2
00:06:38,181 --> 00:06:44,181
Barbie: The Pearl Princess is a family, animation
film directed by Ezekiel Norton.

3
00:09:57,272 --> 00:10:03,272
Barbie plays Lumina, a mermaid girl with the
power to change the color of pearls.

4
00:13:16,363 --> 00:13:22,363
Cheerful and creative, Lumina finds herself working in a
mermaid salon customizing fabulous hairstyles.

5
00:16:35,454 --> 00:16:41,454
And when Lumina has the chance to attend the royal ball, her
friends adorn her with a gown fit for a princess.

6
00:19:54,545 --> 00:20:00,545
At the ball, villains try to seize power over the kingdom, and Lumina finds within
herself an unexpected power that proves she is much more than a hair stylist.

7
00:23:13,636 --> 00:23:19,636
Lumina (voice), played by Kelly Sheridan.

8
00:26:32,727 --> 00:26:38,727
Kuda (voice), played by Katie Crown.

9
00:29:51,818 --> 00:29:57,818
King Nereus (voice), played by Mark Oliver.

10
00:33:10,909 --> 00:33:16,909
Queen Lorelei (voice), played by Rebecca Shoichet.

11
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:36,000
Scylla (voice), played by Trish Pattendon.

12
00:39:49,090 --> 00:39:55,090
Caligo (voice), played by John Novak.

13
00:43:08,181 --> 00:43:14,181
Barbie is an American multimedia franchise created by Mattel based on the
eponymous fashion doll created by its co-founder, Ruth Handler.

14
00:46:27,272 --> 00:46:33,272
It began with the release of an eponymous video game in 1984 and has since been featured in more video games, a
film series and in other media formats across technologies like television and the Internet.

15
00:49:46,363 --> 00:49:52,363
It is currently one of the highest-grossing media franchises of all time and has
been referred to among fans as the "Barbie Cinematic Universe".

16
00:53:05,454 --> 00:53:11,454
The film series were released direct-to-video from 2001 and aired regularly on Nickelodeon as
television specials in the United States from 2002, with both running until early 2017.

17
00:56:24,545 --> 00:56:30,545
Since mid-2017, beginning with Barbie Dolphin Magic, Mattel revamped them into streaming television films, branded or marketed them as animated "specials" with a reduced 1-hour video length
as opposed to the feature films beforehand and moved them to streaming media services, predominantly Netflix, but also including Amazon Prime Video, Google Play and Apple TV+.

18
00:59:43,636 --> 00:59:49,636
These films released from late 2017 onwards could still be released on home video formats, although
locally, or get picked up for television broadcast in multiple countries and regions/territories.

19
01:03:02,727 --> 01:03:08,727
Aside from the film series, Barbie has been featured in other media formats, including two animated direct-to-video specials in Barbie and the Rockers: Out of This World and its sequel, Barbie and the Sensations: Rockin' Back to Earth, both in 1987, web series like Barbie:
Life in the Dreamhouse, Barbie: Vlogger, Barbie Dreamtopia, Barbie's Dreamworld and Barbie: Life in the City, television shows like Barbie Dreamhouse Adventures, Barbie: It Takes Two and  Barbie: A Touch of Magic, a live-action film and an interactive short film.

20
01:06:21,818 --> 01:06:27,818
The film explores themes of princess, magic,
mermaid, based on toy, female protagonist.

21
01:09:40,909 --> 01:09:46,909
The tone is lighthearted.
